# Terraform — never commit these to a public repo.
#
# .terraform/ is the local data directory `terraform init` writes, and here it
# is the expensive one: this stack pulls the aws, tls and random providers, and
# the aws provider binary alone is ~707 MB — seven times GitHub's 100 MB
# per-file limit, so a commit carrying it cannot be pushed at all. It also holds
# .terraform/terraform.tfstate, which is NOT this stack's state (that lives in
# S3, see the backend block in versions.tf) but a cache of the backend
# configuration. Nothing under .terraform/ belongs in the repo; it is rebuilt by
# `terraform init`.
# Note the one difference from the sibling site modules: they also ignore
# .terraform.lock.hcl, but here it is deliberately tracked, so it is not listed
# (a .gitignore entry would not untrack it anyway, only mislead).
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log
*.tfplan
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# terraform.tfvars here holds only the domain, instance size and account names —
# no secret, and allowed_cidr is deliberately absent — so it is committed. The
# domain, service and user passwords are generated per apply by the random
# provider and live only in the remote state.
